---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const projects = [
  {
    href: "/projects/cmux-maestro",
    title: "CMUX Maestro",
    description: "The orchestration plugin — sidebar visibility into what my agents are actually doing.",
  },
  {
    href: "/projects/pr-sniper",
    title: "PR Sniper",
    description: "A menu-bar app so pull requests get human-owned review, not rubber-stamps.",
  },
  {
    href: "/projects/cacophony",
    title: "Cacophony",
    description: "Adversarial PR review by six AI personas, so one agent's code doesn't just get rubber-stamped by another.",
  },
  {
    href: "https://jdylanmc.github.io/topo-code/",
    title: "Topocode",
    description: "Living architecture diagrams generated from the repository itself.",
    external: true,
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Dylan McCurry — I manage an AI now">
  <span class="eyebrow">Hi, I'm Dylan</span>
  <h1>I was a software engineer for 15 years.</h1>
  <h1>
    Then some guy named <span style="color: var(--accent)">Claude</span> took my job.
  </h1>
  <p class="muted" style="font-size: 1.1rem; max-width: 60ch;">
    So I became his manager. Turns out fifteen years of writing code was decent preparation for
    reviewing someone else's — even when that someone else is an AI, works at 3am, and has never
    once asked for a raise.
  </p>

  <div style="margin: 2rem 0; display: flex; gap: 0.75rem; flex-wrap: wrap;">
    <a class="btn" href="/about">The full story</a>
    <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/projects">My environment</a>
  </div>

  <hr />

  <span class="eyebrow">The foundation</span>
  <h2>It all runs on dotfiles</h2>
  <p class="muted">
    Before any of this, Claude had to understand the environment it was running in. So we went
    chunk by chunk — every shell file, every alias, every identity-routing rule — until it could
    explain why each piece is there.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <a class="card" href="/projects/dotfiles">
      <h3>Dotfiles</h3>
      <p class="muted">The environment, chunk by chunk — what every file does and why it's there.</p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <hr />

  <h2>So I had Claude build my digital environment</h2>
  <p class="muted">
    Management, it turns out, means delegating. So Claude built the tooling that keeps himself —
    and every other AI direct report — honest, reviewable, and productive. Each project below has
    its own page.
  </p>

  <div class="card-grid">
    {projects.map((p) => (
      <a class="card" href={p.href} target={p.external ? "_blank" : undefined} rel={p.external ? "noreferrer" : undefined}>
        <h3>{p.title}{p.external ? " ↗" : ""}</h3>
        <p class="muted">{p.description}</p>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>

  <hr />

  <span class="eyebrow">Look what I built, long before AI was a thing</span>
  <h2>The archive (2011, MTSU)</h2>
  <p class="muted">
    Three demos restored to actually run — WebGL particles, a live Wikipedia graph explorer, and
    an interactive US map.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <a class="card" href="/projects/particle-engine">
      <h3>JS Particle Engine</h3>
      <p class="muted">A hand-rolled WebGL particle system from a game engine architecture course.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="/projects/wikipedia-visualization">
      <h3>Wikipedia Visualization</h3>
      <p class="muted">A live 3D force-directed graph of Wikipedia topics, built in grad school.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="/projects/us-map">
      <h3>Interactive US Map</h3>
      <p class="muted">A TopoJSON/D3 map of US states with click-to-highlight and labeled popouts.</p>
    </a>
  </div>
</Layout>
